WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday. So I really loved your video about perspective,

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.420
and in the beginning of it, you were like:

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.200
[Hank]: Everybody write in the comment box - no math allowed, no Google searches -

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.780
how long ago you think it was a million seconds ago.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.760
[John]: And my immediate thought was: "That's easy. Twelve years."

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.960
Of course it turns out that it is in fact, twelve days.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.520
I'm not very good at measuring time, as long-time Nerdfighters will no doubt remember.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.020
Yeah once I acted as if a minute had a hundred seconds in it - it's very embarrassing. Anyway...

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.520
All that made me think about perspective, and where I was a million imagined seconds ago, so here's a story.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.880
I was twenty-four and my long-time girlfriend had just broken up with me,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.780
and I was living in Chicago in what had been OUR apartment

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.060
with what had been OUR cat.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.860
And I was pretty depressed. I thought the depression was a result of having been dumped,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.960
but in retrospect, getting dumped was probably at least partly a result of the depression.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.260
You know, with its incumbent-like obsessive thinking patterns, and self-focus

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.340
and inability to, like, do anything.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.600
Also, I was trying to write a novel, but I hadn't written a sentence I liked in more than a year,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.480
and while I did have, like, a great job at Booklist magazine,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.560
and wonderful colleagues, and friends, and a great family,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.460
I was really losing it.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.220
Hank, to give you some context, a lot of days the only thing I would eat or drink

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.140
would be one 2-litre bottle of Sprite, which is a bad nutrition strategy

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.900
on many levels, including the fact that you lose a lot of weight, and despite the

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.120
lemon and lime flavoring, y- you eventually get scurvy.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.840
But before I got to the scurvy point, I called my parents one night, and we all agreed that I

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.200
needed to come home, so I woke up the next morning, and I quit my job.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.220
Actually, I tried to quit my job, but the publisher of Booklist, Bill Ott, was like

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.400
"Why don't you just take a leave of absence for a couple weeks,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.640
see if you can get well enough to come back to work."

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.440
Which, in retrospect, is one of the nicest things anyone ever did for me.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.520
Also, he gave me a note, which I still have to this day.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.640
"John, I stopped by to say goodbye. Hope all goes well and you're back here

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.700
in two weeks with an appetite that would put a longshoreman to shame.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.100
Now more than ever, watch Harvey. Bill."

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.180
Bill had been bugging me for like three years to watch this old black and white movie, Harvey,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.060
so my dad drove up to Chicago and then we drove back to Orlando together,

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:01.120
I got into daily therapy, I started taking a much better medication for me, AND... I watched Harvey.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.900
Now Hank, as you know, I'm very suspicious of epiphanies,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.900
because I think people have life-changing revelations all the time,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.980
that turn out not to actually change their lives.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.380
That said, all I know is that I woke up the morning after watching Harvey

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.460
feeling a little bit better, and in all the years since, I have never felt

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.720
quite as hopeless as I did before I watched Harvey.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.320
The movie's about this guy, Elwood P. Dowd, whose best friend is a six foot tall, invisible white rabbit,

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.700
and there is one line in the movie that I think really did change my life.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.800
Elwood says: "My mother used to tell me that in this world, Elwood, you must be oh so smart

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.240
or oh so pleasant.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.900
Well for years I was smart.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.420
I recommend pleasant."

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.920
Two weeks later I went back to Booklist, and within six months I handed Eileen Cooper, my mentor,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.020
forty single-spaced pages of the story that eventually became Looking For Alaska.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.660
Now Hank, I don't want to pretend that everything was rosy. I still missed

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.020
my ex-girlfriend, I was still really screwed up, and those forty single-spaced

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.420
pages were pretty terrible.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.760
But with a million seconds of perspective - or I guess 365 million seconds...

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.060
I see a life that I am now very grateful to have, beginning to happen.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.560
But I didn't know that then, Hank. All I knew was that I was a little less hopeless than I had been.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.620
You can't know what an experience will mean to future you, until you are future you.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.020
You need millions of seconds of perspective, which ultimately, only time can buy.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.300
But until then, there is always the gift of Elwood P. Dowd.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.580
There's a great moment in the movie where a mail-man says to Elwood:

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.960
"Beautiful day, isn't it?" and Elwood replies:

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.680
"Every day is a beautiful day."

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.780
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

